PROPOSED, NOT APPLIED. Reviewable diff for the og391-vs-OG-1.1.1 render-tone gap.
================================================================================================

ROOT CAUSE -- two lines in OmniGibson itself, neither an asset
-------------------------------------------------------------
Both stacks funnel every DatasetObject light through the same `gm.FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY` block in
`DatasetObject._initialize()`. Two things changed between versions:

  1. `omnigibson/macros.py`
       OG 1.1.1 (line 153):  gm.FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY = 150000
       OG 3.9.1 (line 275):  gm.FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY = 10000        <-- 15x lower

  2. `omnigibson/objects/dataset_object.py`
       OG 1.1.1 (lines 196-204) sets ONLY `inputs:intensity`.
       OG 3.9.1 (lines 225-236) sets `inputs:intensity` AND, additionally:
           child_prim.GetAttribute("inputs:normalize").Set(True)     <-- new in 3.9.1

`normalize=True` makes emission proportional to intensity/area instead of intensity, and these
lights are small (a rect light of 0.034 x 0.827 m = 0.028 m^2), so dividing by area MULTIPLIES the
effective emission by ~35. Net: 10000 x ~35 vs 1.1.1's 150000 -- og391's lights are roughly 2.4x
brighter than 1.1.1's despite a nominal intensity total measured at **15x lower**
(1,057,715 vs 15,833,736 summed over 108 lights). That contradiction is the fingerprint.

Confirmed by a within-boot algebraic identity on `pick_water_bottle`: `normalize=False` at
intensity x15 reproduces the untouched `normalize=True` render to 0.3 luma
(mean 101.58 vs 101.31, p5 13.4 vs 13.6, %dark 37.90 vs 37.95).

